178: \title[Complex $\bS^6$]{Complex structures on $\bS^6$}
179: \author{Philip Engel}
180: \address{Department of Mathematics, Statistics, and Computer Science,
181: University of Illinois Chicago, 851 S Morgan St, Chicago, IL 60607, USA}
182: \email{pengel@uic.edu}
183: \date{\today}
184: \subjclass[2020]{Primary 32J17; Secondary 14J27, 32Q55, 32G20}
185: 
186: \begin{document}
187: \begin{abstract} 
188: A recent manuscript produced by Claude
189: under the direction of Levent Alp\"oge constructs a 1-parameter family 
190: of compact complex threefolds diffeomorphic to $\bS^6$ \cite{AC26}. 
191: Our goal is to describe the geometric ideas behind the construction,
192: give a self-contained proof, and remark on possible future research directions. \end{abstract}
193: \maketitle
194: 
195: \setcounter{tocdepth}{1}
196: \tableofcontents
197: 
198: 
199: \section{Introduction}\label{sec-intro}
200: 
201: \subsection{History} In 1948, Hopf proved
202: that $\bS^4$ and $\bS^8$ do not admit complex structures
203: \cite{Hop48} and in 1953, Borel
204: and Serre \cite{BS53} used topological methods to 
205: prove that only $\bS^{2}$ and $\bS^6$ admit an almost
206: complex structure. Since $\bS^2\simeq \bP^1$, this raises the question:
207: 
208: \begin{question}[Hopf Problem]\label{hopf-q}
209: Does $\bS^6$ admit a complex structure?
210: \end{question} 
211: 
212: Yau asked whether every compact almost complex manifold
213: of dimension at least $6$ also admits a complex structure \cite[Prob.~52]{Yau93}.
214: 
215: Both negative and positive answers to Question \ref{hopf-q} have
216: been proposed, but these works were
217: found not to be fully mathematically sound. See 
218: \cite{ABGKR18} and \cite{Bry21} for discussions. 
219: Thus, the problem acquired a certain degree of notoriety.
220: Hypothetical properties of such a complex structure, for instance, 
221: Dolbeault cohomology groups and algebraic dimension,
222: were studied in \cite{Gra97}, \cite{CDP98}.
223: 
224: In 2026, L.~Alp\"oge\footnote{The precise nature of the 
225: interaction between Alp\"oge and Claude is not, at present, documented.}
226: posted a 108-page manuscript \cite{AC26} produced by an artificial intelligence,
227: Anthropic's ``Claude'', 
228: claiming to construct a complex 
229: structure on $\bS^6$. Perhaps the most remarkable aspect
230: of the construction is that it fibers holomorphically 
231: over the Riemann sphere $\bP^1$, contradicting
232: a nearly 30-year-old result in the literature that the algebraic dimension of such a complex
233: structure must be zero \cite[Cor.~4.2]{CDP98}. See \cite[\S~10]{AC26} for a further discussion.
234: 
235: \subsection{Outline}
236: 
237: We begin with the rational elliptic surface $S$ having
238: singular fibers of Kodaira types $IV^*$, $III$, $I_1$.  From a line bundle
239: $M\in {\rm Pic}(S)$, one forms a principal $\bG_m$-bundle over $S$, then quotients it by a
240: lift of translation along a section of $S\to \bP^1$. 
241: This linearization acquires a zero over $\infty\in \bP^1$ where one fills the quotient family
242: by a Mumford construction.
243: The result is a compact complex
244: $3$-fold $X(u)$ fibering over $\bP^1$, with complex $2$-torus fibers away from $0,1,\infty$.
245: It depends on a single complex parameter $u\in \Delta^*$. See Section \ref{sec-initial}. 
246: 
247: Using the potential good reduction of $S$ over $0,1\in \bP^1$ to perform
248: logarithmic transforms, one can modify the family over $0,1$. The replaced fibers
249: have multiplicities $3,4$ respectively, and their reductions are bielliptic surfaces. 
250: The result is a compact complex $3$-fold $Y=Y(u)$ fibering over $\bP^1$.
251: See Section \ref{sec-log-mod}. 
252: The monodromy representation of the fibration 
253: $Y\to \bP^1$ is computed in Section \ref{sec-monodromy}.
254: Using this, one checks by van Kampen that $Y$ is simply connected (Section \ref{sec-fd}), 
255: and by the Leray spectral sequence, that $Y$ is a $\bZ$-homology sphere (Section \ref{sec-homology}).
